Imager::File::SIXEL
===================

Reads and writes SIXEL images with Imager. SIXEL is the bitmap graphics
format of DEC terminals that many current terminal emulators understand,
which makes it a common way to show images inside a terminal.

    use Imager;
    use Imager::File::SIXEL;

    my $img = Imager->new(file => 'photo.png') or die Imager->errstr;
    my $sixel = '';
    $img->write(data => \$sixel, type => 'sixel') or die $img->errstr;
    binmode STDOUT, ':raw';
    print $sixel;

The encoder is written in C and, on a current desktop CPU, encodes a
640 x 480 frame in 3 to 16 milliseconds, depending on the settings and
the picture:
fast enough for animations at 60 frames per second. See the module documentation for all options:

    perldoc Imager::File::SIXEL

The examples directory holds three scripts: sixel-cat.pl shows image
files in the terminal, sixel-animate.pl plays an animation, and
sixel-bench.pl measures encoding speed.

INSTALLATION

Imager 1.013 or later, a C compiler and Perl 5.24 or later are
required.

    cpanm --installdeps .
    perl Makefile.PL
    make
    make test
    make install

The author tests in xt/ need Test::Pod:

    prove -b xt/

